"S´eminaire Lotharingien de Combinatoire 91 (2025), Article B91b (https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~slc/wpapers/s91alia.pdf)\nciteturn0search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; THE WEAK ACYCLIC MATCHING PROPERTY IN ABELIAN GROUPS ... prime p? ... answer to Conjecture 1.5 and thoroughly addresses Question 1.6. ... paper is related to the classical theory introduced by Philip Hall in 1935, the two\nTHE WEAK ACYCLIC MATCHING PROPERTY IN ABELIAN GROUPS\n3\nFollowing [2], we deﬁne an abelian group G to possess the weak acyclic matching\nproperty if, for every ﬁnite nonempty subsets A and B of G with |A| = |B| and\nA ∩(A + B) = ∅, A is acyclically matched to B. In [2], the following conjecture\nconcerning the weak acyclic matching property was proposed.\nConjecture 1.5. There are inﬁnitely many n for which Z/nZ has the weak acyclic\nmatching property.\nThe following question was also raised in [1].\nQuestion 1.6. Does Z/pZ possess the weak acyclic matching property for every\nprime p?\nIn this note, our main result, stated as Theorem 2.3, provides an aﬃrmative\nanswer to Conjecture 1.5 and thoroughly addresses Question 1.6.\nFurthermore,\nTheorem 2.3 classiﬁes all abelian groups with respect to their adherence to the weak\nacyclic matching property.\nRemark 1.7. It is worth noting that while the matching theory developed in this\npaper is related to the classical theory introduced by Philip Hall in 1935, the two\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStar edge-coloring of square grids (https://naos-be.zcu.cz/server/api/core/bitstreams/89109ccf-fcd3-4d40-925a-650c06148100/content)\nciteturn0search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.1 years ago; Crawled: 6.1 years ago; **Conjecture 3. ... We expect this phenomenon will repeat also for some cycle \\(C_m\\), where \\(m \\in \\{11, 13, 17\\}\\), although, in the case of \\(C_{11}\\) and \\(C_{17}\\), we found star 6-edge-colorings of \\(C_{11} \\square P_8\\) and \\(C_{17} \\square P_8\\), which could indicate that 6 colors are sufficient in these cases.\n\\(P_2\\). Although we improved existing bounds and proved a number of exact values, there are still some open questions. We are very confident that the following conjecture is true.\n\n**Conjecture 1.** *There exist constants \\(K_1\\) and \\(K_2\\) such that for every pair of integers \\(m\\) and \\(n\\), where \\(m \\ge K_1\\) and \\(n \\ge K_2\\), we have*\n\n\\[\n\\chi'_{st}(C_m \\square P_n)=6.\n\\]\n\nNote that Conjecture 1 is equivalent to the following.\n\n**Conjecture 2.** *There exists a constant \\(K\\) such that for every integer \\(m\\), where \\(m \\ge K\\), there exists an integer \\(n\\) such that*\n\n\\[\n\\chi'_{st}(C_m \\square C_n)=6.\n\\]\n\nIn fact, we believe (with a bit lower confidence) that the following stronger version of Conjecture 1 can also be confirmed.\n\n**Conjecture 3.** *There exists a constant \\(L\\) such that for every pair of integers \\(m\\) and \\(n\\), where \\(m, n \\ge L\\), we have*\n\n\\[\n\\chi'_{st}(C_m \\square C_n)=6.\n\\]\n\nThe above conjectures seem to be challenging, since we were not able to observe any straightforward pattern in colorings of different Cartesian products, despite the help of computer in our constructions.  \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe weak Loneliness Spectrum Conjecture for one very fast speed | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/344879/the-weak-loneliness-spectrum-conjecture-for-one-very-fast-sp)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; # The weak Loneliness Spectrum Conjecture for one very fast speed\n\n# The weak Loneliness Spectrum Conjecture for one very fast speed\n\n7 years old · traced to 2019\n\nLet $n\\geq4$n≥4 be an integer. For positive integers $v_1<\\cdots<v_n$v 1​<⋯<v n​, define\n\n$$\\operatorname{ML}(v_1,\\ldots,v_n)=\\max_{t\\in\\mathbb{R}}\\min_{1\\leq i\\leq n}\\Vert tv_i\\Vert.$$ML(v 1​,…,v n​)=t∈R max​1≤i≤n min​∥t v i​∥.\n\nWeak Loneliness Spectrum Conjecture. For every integer $n\\geq4$n≥4, there exists a function $f_n:\\mathbb{N}\\to\\mathbb{N}$f n​:N→N such that, whenever $v_n>f_n(v_{n-1})$v n​>f n​(v n−1​), either\n\n$$\\operatorname{ML}(v_1,\\ldots,v_n)=\\frac{s}{ns+1}\\quad\\text{for some }s\\in\\mathbb{N},$$ML(v 1​,…,v n​)=n s+1 s​for some s∈N,\n\nor\n\n$$\\operatorname{ML}(v_1,\\ldots,v_n)\\geq\\frac{1}{n}.$$ML(v 1​,…,v n​)≥n 1​.\n\nThis is a regime-specific refinement of the Loneliness Spectrum Conjecture, addressing sets of speeds with one speed sufficiently larger than the preceding one. The paper proves a stronger explicit bound for $n=4$n=4 and $n=6$n=6, but the assertion for every $n\\geq4$n≥4 remains open.\n\nReferences\n\n  1. Noah Kravitz, “Barely lonely runners and very lonely runners”, arXiv:1912.06034 (2019).\n\ncombinatorial-number-theorylonely-runner\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStar Edge-Coloring of Square Grids · ProbXiv (https://probxiv.com/problems/star-edge-coloring-of-square-grids-3)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: last month; By the standard concatenation lemma for included cycle colorings, this yields a star $6$6-edge-coloring of ... The original Holub–Lužar–Mihaliková–Mockovčiaková–Soták paper leaves this as Conjecture 2 and only proves partial exact values and the general $6\\le\\chi'_{\\rm st}(C_m\\square C_n)\\le7$6≤χ st′​(C m​□C n​)≤7. ... Searches through arXiv star-edge-coloring papers, CORE title/metadata searches for square-grid/star-edge-coloring work, OpenAlex metadata/citations, and author arXiv pages did not reveal any later square-grid result or a $C_{13}\\square C_{12}$C 13​□C 12​ construction.\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------